-- Q5791: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-188, differential privacy is a mathematical framework that adds calibrated noise to query results so that the output is statistically indistinguishable whether or not any individual''s record is included. This provides formal privacy guarantees for aggregate data releases.
Wrong (A): Data masking replaces sensitive values with fictional but realistic data. It does not add statistical noise to query results or provide the mathematical privacy guarantees of differential privacy.
Wrong (B): Generalization reduces data precision (e.g., replacing exact ages with ranges) but does not add calibrated noise. It lacks the formal guarantee that individual inclusion is indistinguishable.
Wrong (D): Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with tokens but does not add noise to outputs. Pseudonymized records can still be re-identified through linkage attacks if auxiliary data is available.$EXPL$
